  "description": "A parametric TRACK HEAD on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nDROP IT ON A NODE, on a `track` or on its own. It is the aimed fitting: the two knobs a track head actually has are yaw and pitch, and everything downstream reads the unit vector they make rather than either of them.\n\nPITCH IS MEASURED FROM STRAIGHT DOWN, the same convention `light` and `downlight` use: 0° is a downlight, 85° is nearly horizontal. Three objects measuring the same angle three different ways is how a coordination model goes wrong.\n\nTHE FOOTPRINT IS THE CIRCLE THAT INSCRIBES THE ELLIPSE. A tilted beam lands as an ellipse; this draws the circle where the axis meets the floor with the radius the slant distance gives, and reports `axisReach` so you can see how far the aim has walked it. That is what a lighting plan wants and it is honestly an approximation.\n\nAVERAGE ILLUMINANCE IS ARITHMETIC, NOT A SIMULATION — dimmed lumens over the footprint area. It is a sanity figure for a schedule and it says so.\n\nDIMENSIONS AT THE DEFAULTS: a 110 mm head at 2.65 m pivot, 24° beam pitched 30°, 400 lm at 3000 K — a 1.30 m footprint 1.53 m out, about 300 lx.",
